{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CeymLV4EioKMFTEurzHQm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# How a Bicycle Rim Brake Works

When the rider squeezes the brake lever, they pull a steel cable that runs from the lever to the brake mechanism. This cable is routed along the bicycle frame and connects to the brake caliper. As the cable tightens, it pulls the caliper arms together, creating the mechanical force needed to stop the wheel.

The brake pads, made of friction material, are mounted on the caliper arms and press against the rim when the cable pulls. The friction between the pads and the metal rim creates resistance that slows the wheel's rotation. Releasing the lever allows a spring to push the caliper arms apart, separating the pads from the rim and allowing the wheel to spin freely again."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":64,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":161,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}}

